media: iris: quiesce threaded IRQ before core removal

On linux-qcom-x1e 7.3.0-15.15, removing an initialized Iris device can
deadlock. iris_core_deinit() holds core->lock and calls iris_vpu_power_off(),
whose disable_irq() waits for the threaded IRQ handler. The handler can
be waiting for core->lock in iris_hfi_queue_msg_read(). Hung-task traces
on the deployment host confirm both sides of this cycle.

Hold a runtime-PM reference while synchronizing the IRQ outside core->lock.
Then cancel the system-error work that a finishing response handler could
have scheduled, before deinitializing the core. The nested disable_irq()
in power-off now sees a quiesced IRQ; synchronization is preserved rather
than replaced with a non-waiting disable. Balance the extra PM reference
before destroying the core lock.

This candidate targets device removal. It does not claim to fix the
corresponding lock-order risk in other power-off call paths. Requires
runtime qualification; not installed or loaded.

--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_probe.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_probe.c
@@ -195,17 +195,26 @@
 static void iris_remove(struct platform_device *pdev)
 {
 	struct iris_core *core;
+	int ret;
 
 	core = platform_get_drvdata(pdev);
 	if (!core)
 		return;
 
+	/* The threaded handler takes core->lock; wait before teardown does. */
+	ret = pm_runtime_resume_and_get(core->dev);
+	disable_irq(core->irq);
+	cancel_delayed_work_sync(&core->sys_error_handler);
+
 	iris_core_deinit(core);
 
 	video_unregister_device(core->vdev_dec);
 	video_unregister_device(core->vdev_enc);
 
 	v4l2_device_unregister(&core->v4l2_dev);
+
+	if (ret >= 0)
+		pm_runtime_put_sync(core->dev);
 
 	mutex_destroy(&core->lock);
 }
